# def:
# - The def keyword defines a function in Python.
# - A function groups reusable logic under a name and can accept parameters.
# - Example:
#     def greet(name):
#         print(f"Hello, {name}")
#
# return:
# - The return statement sends a value back to the caller.
# - If no return is present, Python returns None.
# - Example:
#     def add(a, b):
#         return a + b
#
# pass by value vs pass by reference:
# - Python passes references to objects by value.
# - Immutable objects (int, str, tuple) cannot be changed in place.
#   Reassigning a parameter creates a new object local to the function.
# - Mutable objects (list, dict, set) can be modified in place.
#   Changes are visible to the caller because both names refer to the same object.
# - Python is not true pass-by-reference because reassigning a parameter does not
#   change the caller's variable binding.
#
# Example of immutable object behavior:
#     def change_number(x):
#         x = x + 5
#         return x
#
#     n = 10
#     print(change_number(n))  # 15
#     print(n)                # 10
#
# Example of mutable object behavior:
#     def add_item(lst):
#         lst.append(99)
#
#     values = [1, 2, 3]
#     add_item(values)
#     print(values)  # [1, 2, 3, 99]
